# Lesson 4, batch 2: a ruffing finesse against the ace. Dummy's ♣KQJ5 opposite a void; lead the king and throw a loser when East plays low.
title: Ruffing finesse against the ace
id: L04-26
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.8, 3.5
student: S
board: 106
dealer: E
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  JT5    742    964    KQJ5
  E  32     T85    J73    A6432
  S  AKQ974 A63    A852   -
  W  86     KQJ9   KQT    T987

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 17 points and six spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
     not *: You have 20 points counting your void, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 20 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Nine spades with the ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 2 = The ace takes care of one heart; the other two are losers.
  diamonds: 2 = The ace takes care of one, and dummy can ruff your fourth.
  clubs: 0 = You have none.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: You have no clubs, and dummy has the king, queen and jack. How could they help? How many times can you get to dummy?
play:
  1   W HK  N H2  E H5  S HA
      note: Four losers, one too many. Dummy's clubs can take care of that: lead the king, and if East has the ace, you can throw losers without losing the lead. Win the ♥A: you'll throw your other hearts on the clubs.
  2   S S4  W S6  N ST  E S2
      note: Draw a round of trumps, ending in dummy with the ten. Dummy's jack is a second way back.
  3   N CK  E C2  S H3*  W C7
      note: Lead the ♣K.
      H3 key: East played low, so throw a loser. If East has the ace, the king wins.
      H3 not *: Did you think about throwing a loser? If East has the ace, the king wins and you're still in dummy. Ruffing it wastes the king. Throw a heart.
  4   N CQ  E CA  S S7  W C8
      note: The king won. Now the queen: this time East covers with the ace, and you ruff. Dummy's jack is a winner.
  5   S S9  W S8  N SJ  E S3
      note: Back to dummy with the ♠J. Trumps are drawn too.
  6   N CJ  E C3  S H6  W C9
      note: Throw your other heart on the jack.
  7   N D4  E D3  S DA  W DT
      note: Now the diamonds. You have two losers, and dummy can ruff your fourth.
  8   S D2  W DK  N D6  E D7
  9   W HQ  N H4  E H8  S SQ
  10  S D5  W DQ  N D9  E DJ
  11  W HJ  N H7  E HT  S SK
  12  S D8  W H9  N S5  E C4
      note: Dummy ruffs your last diamond.
  13  N C5  E C6  S SA  W CT
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many, and dummy's clubs offered
  a way to throw your heart losers away.

  This is a ruffing finesse against the ace. When you lead the king, East
  has to decide first. If East covers, you ruff, and dummy's queen and jack
  are winners. If East plays low, you throw a loser and the king wins. Either
  way you keep the lead. You needed two ways back to dummy, so you used
  dummy's ten and jack of trumps. If West had held the ace, the king would
  have lost to it, but you'd have thrown a heart on it, and the queen would
  still have taken your other heart: 4♠ made just the same.
